The "exactly" comes from what quantifiers *do* in the case of quantified
variables. In Lojban, quantifiers are exact, such that if you say .i mi
dasni pa cutci when you're really wearing two, you're *lying*. You can
verify the CLL to check, but I know this to be true, so I won't bother
looking it up for you.

Anyway, what I was trying to say when I said that they were very different
is simply a refutation of your previous statement, namely:
"pa plise = pa lo plise = "one of one or more things which actually is an
apple" = an apple (as opposed to "that apple")"
This is entirely false, as {pa plise} is most certainly *not* equivalent to
{pa lo plise}. As latro'a further pointed out, the semantics are so
drastically different that such a slip-up can lead to saying something
*completely untrue.*
Syntactically -- and only syntactically -- are these two forms similar.

.i mi'e la tsani mu'o

